","details":{"publishers":["Doubleday"],"identifiers":{"goodreads":["318241"],"librarything":["463216"]},"subtitle":"an ancient Irish guide to leadership","ia_box_id":["IA137502"],"isbn_10":["0385513135"],"pagination":"vi, 50 p. ;","covers":[242738],"ia_loaded_id":["counselsofcormac00corm"],"lc_classifications":["PB1421 .T43 2004"],"key":"/books/OL3301913M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL6972200A","name":"Cormac Mac Airt King of Ireland"},{"key":"/authors/OL401115A","name":"Thomas F. Cleary"}],"publish_places":["New York"],"contributions":["Cormac Mac Airt, King of Ireland, fl. 227-260.","Cleary, Thomas F., 1949-"],"lccn":["2004041317"],"uri_descriptions":["Contributor biographical information","Sample text","Publisher description"],"edition_name":"1st ed.","genres":["Translations into English.","Early works to 1800."],"title":"The counsels of Cormac","url":["http://www.loc.gov/catdir/bios/random051/2004041317.html","http://www.loc.gov/catdir/samples/random051/2004041317.html","http://www.loc.gov/catdir/description/random051/2004041317.html"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Attributed to Cormac Mac Airt.\n\"A new translation from the original old Irish.\""},"number_of_pages":50,"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"dewey_decimal_class":["398.9/9162"],"subjects":["Cormac Mac Airt, King of Ireland, fl. 227-260.","Maxims, Irish -- Translations into English.","Proverbs, Irish -- Translations into English.","Kings and rulers -- Duties -- Early works to 1800."],"publish_date":"2004","publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"Thomas Cleary.","work_title":["Tecosca Cormaic."],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL16064865W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"uris":["http://www.loc.gov/catdir/bios/random051/2004041317.html","http://www.loc.gov/catdir/samples/random051/2004041317.html","http://www.loc.gov/catdir/description/random051/2004041317.html"],"source_records":["marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part31.utf8:173722347:1349","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/ab.bib.09.20150123.full.mrc:480488821:1085","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_006.bib.mrc:266401423:3849"],"oclc_numbers":["54046308"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"\"Celtic lore is replete with legends recounting the contact and cooperation between the ancient Irish peoples and the other great cultures of antiquity (including the Minoan, Egyptian, Greek, and Hebrew) and their influence on Celtic ideas and values. Knowledge was highly prized by the Celts, and their pursuit of learning encompassed everything from seeking an understanding of the ordinary to contemplating the inexplicable. The emphasis on education extended to the selection of leaders: elected by the freeholders of each territory, kings were expected to be well schooled in all branches of knowledge. Traditionally, manuals of instructions were written to ensure the cultivation of people capable of leadership. The Counsels of Cormac, attributed to King Cormac MacAirt, who ruled in the third century C.E., is one of the best-known classics of this tradition.\" \"Cormac, portrayed by Irish poets and historians as one of the greatest of the Irish high kings, is particularly famed for his achievements in culture and for the personal qualities he brought to governing. In the words of a later historian he was \"wise, learned, valiant and mild, not given causelessly to be bloody as many of his ancestors were; he reigned majestically and magnificently.\"\" \"Thomas Cleary's highly readable contemporary English translation of The Counsels of Cormac brings the legendary king's sage advice to present-day readers. From a to-the-point chapter outlining the \"traditional prescription for a chieftain\" to a charming discourse on \"what is fitting for a chieftain and an alehouse\" (a Celtic version of how to create a productive and pleasurable workplace), The Counsels of Cormac is perfect for those seeking to enhance their own leadership abilities, learn from the wisdom of the past, and connect with the roots of Celtic civilization.\"--Jacket."},"latest_revision":9,"revision":9,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-06-30T10:55:51.240820"}}},"ISBN:9781570622748":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9781570622748","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL1004660M/Living_a_good_life","preview":"restricted","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/livinggoodlifead0000unse","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/817199-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Shambhala","Distributed in the U.S. by Random House"],"identifiers":{"librarything":["2509203"],"goodreads":["494968"]},"subtitle":"advice on virtue, love, and action from the ancient Greek masters","isbn_10":["1570622744"],"covers":[817199],"lc_classifications":["BJ1595 .L75 1997","BJ1595.L75 1997"],"key":"/books/OL1004660M","publish_places":["Boston","[New York]"],"contributions":["Cleary, Thomas F., 1949-"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"pagination":"xi, 116 p. ;","source_records":["bwb:9781570622748","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part25.utf8:107996848:969","ia:livinggoodlifead0000unse","promise:bwb_daily_pallets_2020-11-27"],"title":"Living a good life","dewey_decimal_class":["170/.44"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references (p. 115-116)."},"number_of_pages":116,"edition_name":"1st ed.","lccn":["96044398"],"subjects":["Conduct of life.","Ethics, Ancient."],"publish_date":"1997","publish_country":"mau","by_statement":"translated from the Arabic by Thomas Cleary.","oclc_numbers":["36259429"],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL16466769W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"ocaid":"livinggoodlifead0000unse","local_id":["urn:bwbsku:O5-KOW-431"],"latest_revision":11,"revision":11,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2023-01-15T12:12:15.097410"}}},"ISBN:9781570622694":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9781570622694","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL8697429M/Kensho","preview":"restricted","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781570622694","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/817195-S.jpg","details":{"identifiers":{"librarything":["83587"],"goodreads":["495924"]},"title":"Kensho","subtitle":"The Heart of Zen (Shambhala Dragon Editions)","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL2632324A","name":"Thomas Cleary"}],"publish_date":"January 21, 1997","publishers":["Shambhala"],"weight":"8 ounces","isbn_10":["1570622698"],"covers":[817195],"local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223053512936","urn:sfpl:31223044698224","urn:bwbsku:W8-BTX-259"],"physical_format":"Paperback","lc_classifications":["BQ9288.K46 1997","BQ9288 .K46 1997"],"isbn_13":["9781570622694"],"source_records":["marc:marc_records_scriblio_net/part25.dat:180819554:737","marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run02.mrc:150563670:1751","bwb:9781570622694","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part25.utf8:86761113:838","promise:bwb_daily_pallets_2023-05-22:W8-BTX-259","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-004.mrc:573189398:1143","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_034.bib.mrc:276697202:3002"],"lccn":["96018728"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"subjects":["Zen Buddhism","Religion - World Religions","Philosophy","China","Japan","Korea","Buddhism - Zen","Zen","Religion / Zen Buddhism","Enlightenment (Zen Buddhism)","Spiritual life"],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"physical_dimensions":"8.8 x 5.9 x 0.5 inches","ocaid":"isbn_9781570622694","key":"/books/OL8697429M","number_of_pages":123,"works":[{"key":"/works/OL19050521W"}],"oclc_numbers":["34912592"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Kensho is the transformative glimpse of the true nature of all things. It is an experience so crucial in Zen practice that it is sometimes compared to finding an inexhaustible treasure because it reveals the potential that exists in each moment for pure awareness free from the projections of the ego. Among the traditional Zen works are a number of important texts focusing on the profound subtleties of this essential Zen awakening and the methods used in its realization. The selections here are taken from: Straightforward Explanation of the True Mind, by Korean Zen teacher Chinul (1158-210), which provides the contextual balance needed to understand kensho by relating it to the broader teachings of the Buddhist scriptures and treatises; several works by Japanese Zen master Hakuin (1786-1769), whose teachings emphasize the techniques used in the cultivation and application of kensho and the importance of going beyond the experience itself to apply Zen insight to the full range of human endeavors; and The Book of Ease, a Chinese koan collection from the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, with commentary showing the practical dimension of classical koan practice. The translator provides extensive introductory notes and detailed commentary on each of the selections to help the reader understand the inner meaning of this essential experience of Zen."},"latest_revision":14,"revision":14,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-30T08:14:56.482104"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-04-06T07:47:34.698021"}}},"ISBN:9781570620188":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9781570620188","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL1120220M/Buddhist_yoga","preview":"restricted","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/buddhistyogacomp0000unse","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/817024-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Shambhala","Distributed in the U.S. by Random House"],"identifiers":{"librarything":["254521"],"goodreads":["327648"]},"subtitle":"a comprehensive course","covers":[817024],"lc_classifications":["BQ2092.E5 C54 1995","BQ2092.E5C54 1995"],"key":"/books/OL1120220M","publish_places":["Boston","[New York]"],"contributions":["Cleary, Thomas F., 1949-"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"pagination":"xi, 109 p. ;","source_records":["marc:marc